<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Holy Grail</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Holy Grail is a vessel of medieval legend, identified with the cup used by Christ at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/last-supper">Last Supper</a> or the dish that caught his blood at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crucifixion">Crucifixion</a>. It first appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a>'s romance Perceval around 1190 and became central to Arthurian literature. Several real objects were venerated as the Grail, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacro-catino">Sacro Catino</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and the Holy Chalice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holy-grail.jpg" alt="Holy Grail" width="320" height="513" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dante Gabriel Rossetti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dante_Gabriel_Rossetti_-_The_Damsel_of_the_Sanct_Grael_(1874).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word graal comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> graal or greal, related to Old Occitan grazal and Old Catalan gresal, meaning a cup or bowl of earth, wood or metal. The most widely accepted derivation is from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> gradalis, via cratalis, from the Greek krater, a large wine-mixing vessel. In the 15th century John Hardyng invented a derivation from sang réal, royal blood, which Thomas Malory used and which later fed the theory of a bloodline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>.</p><p>Scholars disagree about where the idea came from. Roger Sherman Loomis, Alfred Nutt and Jessie Weston looked to Celtic myth, such as the magical cauldrons of Welsh tradition and Bran the Blessed in the Mabinogion. Others argue for a Christian origin; Joseph Goering points to 12th-century wall paintings from churches in the Catalan Pyrenees showing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> holding a bowl radiating tongues of fire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The first grail appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a>'s unfinished Perceval, the Story of the Grail, dated between 1180 and 1191. Perceval, a guest in the castle of the Fisher King, sees a procession in which a girl carries a decorated graal, a serving dish, and a bleeding lance is borne before him. He stays silent, and later a hermit tells him that the grail holds a host that keeps the crippled king alive; had he asked the right questions he would have healed the king and restored the Wasteland.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfram-von-eschenbach">Wolfram von Eschenbach</a>'s Parzival makes the Grail a gemstone, lapis exillis, the sanctuary of the neutral angels in Lucifer's rebellion. In Robert de Boron's Joseph d'Arimathie, composed between 1191 and 1202, the Grail becomes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/last-supper">Last Supper</a> chalice in which Joseph of Arimathea caught Christ's blood; Joseph then carries it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The Lancelot-Grail cycle introduced Galahad as its hero, who achieves the Grail in the Queste del Saint Graal. In the German Diu Crône it is Gawain who does so.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Relics later came to be identified with the Grail, though traditions of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/last-supper">Last Supper</a> relic stayed rare beside those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> and Holy Lance. The pilgrim Arculf reported a Last Supper chalice near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the 7th century, before the romances. Glastonbury became tied to the legend, and from the 15th century the Grail was prominent in its stories.</p><p>In 1818 Joseph von Hammer-Purgstall linked the Grail with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>, though no historical evidence connects the order with a search for it, and in 1906 Joséphin Péladan identified the Cathar castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montsegur">Montségur</a> with the Grail castle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a>'s Parsifal premiered in 1882, and Tennyson's Idylls of the King and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-s-eliot">T. S. Eliot</a>'s The Waste Land of 1922 drew on the legend.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Holy Grail” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1930s Otto Rahn linked them to the <b>Holy Grail</b>, and popular culture has linked them to the Knights Templar.</blockquote><a href="/religion/catharism">Catharism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Baldwin I took Caesarea in May 1101 with a Genoese fleet, which received a share of the spoils including a green glass bowl believed to be the <b>Holy Grail</b>, now the Sacro Catino in Genoa.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-caesarea">Lordship of Caesarea</a></li></ul></section>
